<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<phonemap description="ASCII soft reads: keyboard characters read as IPA on wild import">
    <!--
    Keyboard characters that stand in for an IPA symbol in text written
    outside house style. These are "soft reads": they are applied ONLY by
    the explicit wild-import door (IPAFeatures.from_wild / ipakit.from_wild,
    and the public ipakit.normalize_lookalikes), never by default parsing.
    Default parsing is strict house style, so an ASCII character that is not
    registered IPA stays unknown and is reported as such (docs/ties.md).

    A character earns a row here only if it has one dominant reading in the
    wild. Ambiguous characters are deliberately absent (see below).
    -->

    <map ipa="ɡ" lookalike="g"/>      <!-- U+0261 LATIN SMALL LETTER SCRIPT G <- U+0067 LATIN SMALL LETTER G; pure visual lookalike -->
    <map ipa="ː" lookalike=":"/>      <!-- U+02D0 MODIFIER LETTER TRIANGULAR COLON <- U+003A COLON; length, per Kirshenbaum and X-SAMPA -->
    <map ipa="ʔ" lookalike="?"/>      <!-- U+0294 LATIN LETTER GLOTTAL STOP <- U+003F QUESTION MARK; glottal stop, per Kirshenbaum and X-SAMPA -->
    <map ipa="ˈ" lookalike="'"/>      <!-- U+02C8 MODIFIER LETTER VERTICAL LINE <- U+0027 APOSTROPHE; PRIMARY STRESS, not the ejective U+02BC.
                                           ASCII conventions agree: kirshenbaum.xml already maps ' -> ˈ, and X-SAMPA spells the ejective _> .
                                           Anyone writing the ejective reaches for ʼ directly, so stress is the dominant wild reading. -->

    <!--
    Deliberately NOT mapped:

    "!"  three-way ambiguous with no dominant reading - the alveolar click ǃ
         (U+01C3) in Khoisan/Nguni orthography, DOWNSTEP ꜜ (U+A71C, registered
         here as a suprasegmental) in Africanist tone notation and in X-SAMPA,
         and ordinary sentence punctuation everywhere else. Guessing turns a
         full stop into a consonant, so "!" stays unknown: validate_ipa reports
         it and strict parsing raises on it. Write ǃ or ꜜ to say which.
    -->
</phonemap>
